#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "ec516fb4-a497-579c-b19d-ebc0a081eb58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5822" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1)$.\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u} &= (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n3u(u + 1) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u}) &= 3u(u + 1) \\cdot (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u(u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u(u + 1))/(3u) &= (15u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u \\cdot (u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u \\cdot (u + 1))/(3u) &= (15 \\cdot u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15 \\\\\n9u - u - 1 &= 15 \\\\\n8u &= 16 \\\\\nu &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n2\\neq -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/id #uuid "a578b895-ea0e-5d83-aa1d-04a2fe15f332" :question/math-academy-id "e-5824" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3)$.\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2} &= (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2}) &= (k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2 \\cdot (k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2 \\cdot (k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2 \\\\\n3k - 6 - 2k + 6 &= 2 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083049 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "3384b27a-d977-5421-800e-64a3581b939d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9367" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}$." :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2)$.\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2)) &= \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\nx(x - 2) \\cdot (\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2))) &= x(x - 2) \\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3x - 6 \\\\\n4x - 3x &= -6 + 8 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d04118f4-a490-5479-9c81-a487b5c48d0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263418} {:answer/id #uuid "3d82ef08-e7a5-5f13-a8b2-b4f5e9ebf104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263419} {:answer/id #uuid "ac93a58e-46dc-504d-baf0-cc898100a84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263420} {:answer/id #uuid "77a713fe-34fc-5e46-b6db-cc4105a6e12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263421} {:answer/id #uuid "acfd5b5a-5b72-5f96-b27c-0806cb8be9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac93a58e-46dc-504d-baf0-cc898100a84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263420} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb2d30b-93d4-55b9-ad80-9bc2646a127f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263417}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2976a7b7-c5e4-571a-adf1-1879b7fb12b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117633" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}-5w}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{w(w-5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w-5,\\,3w,\\,w(w-5))=3w(w-5).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=0$ and $w=5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{w(w-5)} \\\\ 3w(w-5)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}) & =3w(w-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{w(w-5)} \\\\ \\frac{6w(w-5)}{w-5}-\\frac{3w(w-5)}{3w}=\\frac{3w(w-5)}{w(w-5)} \\\\ \\frac{6w\\cancel{(w-5)}}{\\cancel{w-5}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3w}(w-5)}{\\cancel{3w}}=\\frac{3\\cancel{w(w-5)}}{\\cancel{w(w-5)}} \\\\ 6w-(w-5) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w-(w-5) & =3 \\\\ 5w+5 & =3 \\\\ 5w & =-2 \\\\ w & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0$ and $-\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 5,$ we conclude that $w=-\\frac{2}{5}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $w=-\\frac{2}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa329ace-559c-5888-a6a1-8bf9aa8b22f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263425} {:answer/id #uuid "a5715624-6a1c-520f-ad76-fdd4edb736fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263426} {:answer/id #uuid "2b88df5c-8220-5af5-a215-ae82ddc1184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263427} {:answer/id #uuid "8d059fbc-6802-5187-9fae-6eb45023b82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263428} {:answer/id #uuid "6af7b969-59ef-5c2d-b664-f2e8fb81ec88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa329ace-559c-5888-a6a1-8bf9aa8b22f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263425} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec110647-ce30-59a7-a377-7075e64038b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263424}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3438a3b3-b047-58a7-ab55-d4def349021a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117634" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}=\\frac{2}{2{V}^{2}+V}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}=\\frac{2}{V(2V+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2V+1,\\,V,\\,V(2V+1))=V(2V+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=0$ and $V=-\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V} & =\\frac{2}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ V(2V+1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}) & =V(2V+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ \\frac{2V(2V+1)}{2V+1}-\\frac{V(2V+1)}{V} & =\\frac{2V(2V+1)}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ \\frac{2V\\cancel{(2V+1)}}{\\cancel{2V+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{V}(2V+1)}{\\cancel{V}} & =\\frac{2\\cancel{V(2V+1)}}{\\cancel{V(2V+1)}} \\\\ 2V-(2V+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2V-(2V+1) & =2 \\\\ 2V-2V-1 & =2 \\\\ -1 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016ef3cc-7d6e-56c4-8b26-7ce1164c6dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263432} {:answer/id #uuid "54a0baeb-d794-51da-83b9-446a4b0369b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263433} {:answer/id #uuid "408557e6-173c-50ce-87df-15446b7c4752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263434} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2cdf5f-d77a-5ff4-8a1e-87d171e12c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263435} {:answer/id #uuid "a198d35e-c8a2-541d-ab6f-5ee29a4dbd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016ef3cc-7d6e-56c4-8b26-7ce1164c6dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263432} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9599e57-b1c0-5efe-9915-6354bc652808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "add7ad15-a989-5880-8090-8af6c15fef69" :question/math-academy-id "q-117682" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(V+4),\\,V-4,\\,(V+4)(V-4))=2(V+4)(V-4).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=-4$ and $V=4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4} & =\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ 2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}) & =2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot \\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{V-4} & =\\frac{16(V+4)(V-4)}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(V+4)}(V-4)}{\\cancel{2(V+4)}}+\\frac{2(V+4)\\cancel{(V-4)}}{\\cancel{V-4}} & =\\frac{16\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}}{\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}} \\\\ (V-4)+2(V+4) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(V-4)+2(V+4) & =16 \\\\ V-4+2V+8 & =16 \\\\ 3V & =12 \\\\ V & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $V=4$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3194bcaa-9628-5ad2-b02d-746f11462748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263439} {:answer/id #uuid "2d651f2f-70eb-5868-8ef2-7c5e57e6ef28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263440} {:answer/id #uuid "180250f0-9f6f-5b85-b070-7d255357f033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263441} {:answer/id #uuid "f728e3de-45a2-5c75-9098-47af5287782c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263442} {:answer/id #uuid "28a68a84-6bb6-54b8-94ec-364e5033432d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f728e3de-45a2-5c75-9098-47af5287782c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263442} :answer-field/id #uuid "563eab5f-3e1b-56ee-9833-f8fbfb52a103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54fb83dc-f3f4-5e13-91b2-57b93c857b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-117688" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+3s-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(s+4),\\,s-1,\\,(s+4)(s-1)))=2(s+4)(s-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $s=-4$ and $s=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(s+4)} & =\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)} \\\\ 2(s+4)(s-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2(s+4)} & =2(s+4)(s-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)}) \\\\ \\frac{2(s+4)(s-1)}{2(s+4)} & =\\frac{2(s+4)(s-1)}{s-1}-\\frac{6(s+4)(s-1)}{(s+4)(s-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(s+4)}(s-1)}{\\cancel{2(s+4)}} & =\\frac{2(s+4)\\cancel{(s-1)}}{\\cancel{s-1}}-\\frac{6\\cancel{(s+4)(s-1)}}{\\cancel{(s+4)(s-1)}} \\\\ (s-1) & =2(s+4)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-1) & =2(s+4)-6 \\\\ s-1 & =2s+2 \\\\ s & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-3\\ne -4$ and $-3\\ne 1,$ we conclude that $s=-3$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $s=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c970dcde-364f-57f9-b7a0-4d8ff3d2d748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263446} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c36966-7cf7-5243-818f-b2f529af8923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263447} {:answer/id #uuid "933848bf-ee90-5eff-9012-588db264987d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263448} {:answer/id #uuid "d6156905-92d6-5588-bbb0-d0047f992f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263449} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd33a56-131d-5cd1-ad6b-38addad54961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c36966-7cf7-5243-818f-b2f529af8923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263447} :answer-field/id #uuid "729cd202-ba72-5c62-87e7-7741f2063259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebbc9b93-1129-5c70-81b6-6537001beeae" :question/math-academy-id "q-118114" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{{d}^{2}-3d}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(d,\\,d(d-3),\\,d-3)=d(d-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $d=0$ and $d=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ d(d-3)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}) & =d(d-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4d(d-3)}{d}+\\frac{6d(d-3)}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2d(d-3)}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{d}\\cdot (d-3)}{\\cancel{d}}+\\frac{6\\cdot \\cancel{d(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d(d-3)}} & =\\frac{2d\\cdot \\cancel{(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d-3}} \\\\ 4(d-3)+6 & =2d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $d,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(d-3)+6 & =2d \\\\ 4d-6 & =2d \\\\ 2d & =6 \\\\ d & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $d=3$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500649fa-0817-500e-a0db-c949999987cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263453} {:answer/id #uuid "2525d91b-2a9b-560a-a114-f449feba69ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263454} {:answer/id #uuid "f171751b-e848-5384-896c-9295d4b81c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263455} {:answer/id #uuid "061e70aa-8ba8-542b-a086-65dd3a8febfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263456} {:answer/id #uuid "a25fb717-985f-56c3-a82a-88873a315a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "500649fa-0817-500e-a0db-c949999987cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263453} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ba2d76-4c27-5934-9c5b-d4d4f30de2c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263452}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a291c84f-37d6-5b32-8ee9-e8a31f9a7934" :question/math-academy-id "q-118166" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}=\\frac{1}{b-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(b,\\,b(b-3),\\,b-3)=b(b-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $b=0$ and $b=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)} & =\\frac{1}{b-3} \\\\ b(b-3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}) & =b(b-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{b-3} \\\\ \\frac{b(b-3)}{b}-\\frac{7b(b-3)}{b(b-3)} & =\\frac{b(b-3)}{b-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{b}\\cdot (b-3)}{\\cancel{b}}-\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{b(b-3)}}{\\cancel{b(b-3)}} & =\\frac{b\\cdot \\cancel{(b-3)}}{\\cancel{b-3}} \\\\ (b-3)-7 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $b,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(b-3)-7 & =b \\\\ b-10 & =b \\\\ -10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above resulted in a false statement, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20d10e2-68cd-5b00-8d2c-2e882af681dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263460} {:answer/id #uuid "a6eaac11-9434-5142-8fbd-ff98ef7cfadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263461} {:answer/id #uuid "77dd4d36-d7f9-55ab-9363-f4baefafece7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263462} {:answer/id #uuid "ca75f145-4a27-5b15-a9eb-0e9cd7784c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263463} {:answer/id #uuid "41f68b4a-21b2-5603-a9a7-0de323f0fc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6eaac11-9434-5142-8fbd-ff98ef7cfadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263461} :answer-field/id #uuid "602ab292-9e46-5e05-84b2-37106af2a5ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9775c2d4-26dd-50d9-aa04-82f75dfe0427" :question/math-academy-id "q-14886" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{20}{{n}^{2}-9}=\\frac{3}{n-3}-\\frac{4}{n+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{(n-3)(n+3)}=\\frac{3}{n-3}-\\frac{4}{n+3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n-3,\\,n+3,\\,(n-3)(n+3))=(n-3)(n+3).\n$$\n\n- The values $n=-3$ and $n=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{(n-3)(n+3)} & =\\frac{3}{n-3}-\\frac{4}{n+3} \\\\ (n-3)(n+3)\\cdot \\frac{20}{(n-3)(n+3)} & =(n-3)(n+3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{n-3}-\\frac{4}{n+3}) \\\\ \\frac{20(n-3)(n+3)}{(n-3)(n+3)} & =\\frac{3(n-3)(n+3)}{n-3}-\\frac{4(n-3)(n+3)}{n+3} \\\\ \\frac{20\\cdot \\cancel{(n-3)(n+3)}}{\\cancel{(n-3)(n+3)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{(n-3)}\\cdot (n+3)}{\\cancel{n-3}}-\\frac{4(n-3)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)}}{\\cancel{n+3}} \\\\ 20 & =3(n+3)-4(n-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =3(n+3)-4(n-3) \\\\ 20 & =3n+9-4n+12 \\\\ 20 & =-n+21 \\\\ n & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\ne -3$ and $1\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $n=1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $n=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7840a230-6a23-5bf9-92a2-d629c386a1ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263467} {:answer/id #uuid "5276abfb-9a18-586e-8ff0-6f2784e5927e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5276abfb-9a18-586e-8ff0-6f2784e5927e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263468} :answer-field/id #uuid "33bad834-b414-5d19-9519-104c3ed97282" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263466}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "483f0885-134e-5728-b968-892c5392a4b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-270924" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-1}-\\frac{2}{x+1}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac{5}{(x-1)(x+1)}-\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x-1,\\,x+1,\\,(x-1)(x+1))=(x+1)(x-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=-1$ and $x=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1} & =\\frac{5}{(x-1)(x+1)}-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ (x+1)(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} & =(x+1)(x-1)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{(x+1)(x-1)}-\\frac{2}{x+1}) \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x-1} & =\\frac{5(x+1)(x-1)}{(x+1)(x-1)}-\\frac{2(x+1)(x-1)}{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(x-1)}}{\\cancel{x-1}} & =\\frac{5\\cancel{(x+1)(x-1)}}{\\cancel{(x+1)(x-1)}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{x+1}} \\\\ x+1 & =5-2(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =5-2(x-1) \\\\ x+1 & =5-2x+2 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $2\\ne -1$ and $2\\ne 1,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c54ef1dc-a113-5a55-b347-7a225ee81a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263471} {:answer/id #uuid "67f3020d-5728-5e8c-9d56-14299c527427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67f3020d-5728-5e8c-9d56-14299c527427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263472} :answer-field/id #uuid "e370c0f2-7b62-53d6-aa27-c9868af45366" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263470}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b8e89a0-6a6d-5b9e-b505-b40b9b084524" :question/math-academy-id "q-276459" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}=\\frac{2}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n+4,\\,n(n+4),\\,n)=n(n+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $n=-4$ and $n=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2}{n} \\\\ n(n+4)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}) & =n(n+4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n(n+4)}{n+4}-\\frac{5n(n+4)}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2n(n+4)}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n\\cdot \\cancel{(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n+4}}-\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{n(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n(n+4)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot (n+4)}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ 3n-5 & =2(n+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-5 & =2(n+4) \\\\ 3n-5 & =2n+8 \\\\ n & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $13\\ne 0$ and $13\\ne -4,$ we conclude that $n=13$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $n=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bae22c6-36de-5346-b378-cbf5ac668d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263475} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3245f-bda8-5056-b559-ce5d6d7ecb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bae22c6-36de-5346-b378-cbf5ac668d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263475} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55c931f-d44e-5d08-9bbd-2b8ee9e92046" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263474}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00aca9fa-13e9-5687-b118-58f599f358ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-276463" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+3m-10}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2}=\\frac{3}{(m+5)(m-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+5,\\,m-2,\\,(m+5)(m-2))=(m+5)(m-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-5$ and $m=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2} & =\\frac{3}{(m+5)(m-2)} \\\\ (m+5)(m-2)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2}) & =(m+5)(m-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+5)(m-2)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+5)(m-2)}{m+5}+\\frac{(m+5)(m-2)}{m-2} & =\\frac{3(m+5)(m-2)}{(m+5)(m-2)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+5)}(m-2)}{\\cancel{m+5}}+\\frac{(m+5)\\cancel{(m-2)}}{\\cancel{m-2}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+5)(m-2)}}{\\cancel{(m+5)(m-2)}} \\\\ 3(m-2)+(m+5) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-2)+(m+5) & =3 \\\\ 4m-1 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =4 \\\\ m & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\ne -5$ and $1\\ne 2,$ we conclude that $m=1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af4db8cd-ed49-5ea9-b50e-0d053baab46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263479} {:answer/id #uuid "e14d80a2-0397-58e9-8a76-5749a1dd84d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e14d80a2-0397-58e9-8a76-5749a1dd84d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263480} :answer-field/id #uuid "682de764-2b08-508b-bebb-81cac51c8686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263478}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "561f7a38-14ce-5b18-9a77-8971c0a37b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-276464" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2{A}^{2}+4A}.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2A,\\,A+2,\\,2A(A+2))=2A(A+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $A=0$ and $A=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2} & =\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ 2A(A+2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}) & =2A(A+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 2A(A+2)}{2A}+\\frac{4A(A+2)}{A+2} & =\\frac{2A(A+2)}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2A}(A+2)}{\\cancel{2A}}+\\frac{4A\\cancel{(A+2)}}{\\cancel{A+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}}{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}} \\\\ 5(A+2)+4A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(A+2)+4A & =1 \\\\ 5A+10+4A & =1 \\\\ 9A & =-9 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-1\\ne 0$ and $-1\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $A=-1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $A=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b668c2a-acab-5342-8c7e-2f57d1cf6242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263483} {:answer/id #uuid "65502db6-8764-58d3-bb29-4dd96d55cc4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263484} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8911dd-b6a7-5eac-8b09-9bd16e4ed67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263485} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ca0f20-f2f5-50e2-9b06-da97ee1aca9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263486} {:answer/id #uuid "d188bd05-6a57-5c3f-bf47-8b68ea79eac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8911dd-b6a7-5eac-8b09-9bd16e4ed67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263485} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7495f67-83a2-5cc2-b163-ccaac12d2a55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98665a91-7482-5e31-8fa9-4f2c01c9be8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98697" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4x-2,\\,x(x-2),\\,x)=x(x-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=2$ and $x=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ x(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)}) & =x(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x(x-2)}{x-2}-\\frac{6x(x-2)}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3x(x-2)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}}-\\frac{6\\cdot \\cancel{x(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x(x-2)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{x}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 4x-6 & =3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6 & =3(x-2) \\\\ 4x-6 & =3x-6 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b88152f8-22c4-57a7-96bd-872ad9ec8332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263490} {:answer/id #uuid "9e86c3d2-de45-5b06-9739-5b7a4ef0ce50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263491} {:answer/id #uuid "266c7336-32cc-5b78-b443-49222b81add3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263492} {:answer/id #uuid "cb390451-d246-5c9d-b90d-0f2918e26521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263493} {:answer/id #uuid "7f00494d-faf1-5d66-89a5-9c61eb60f08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e86c3d2-de45-5b06-9739-5b7a4ef0ce50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263491} :answer-field/id #uuid "482a4ce5-0f5e-5043-b744-982008e9ec0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263489}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d934f63d-6522-5764-bf27-73e6bf757805" :question/math-academy-id "q-98699" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}=\\frac{1}{w-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,\\,w(w-3),\\,w-3)=w(w-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=0$ and $w=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)} & =\\frac{1}{w-3} \\\\ w(w-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}) & =w(w-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{w-3} \\\\ \\frac{2w(w-3)}{w}+\\frac{3w(w-3)}{w(w-3)} & =\\frac{w(w-3)}{w-3} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{w}(w-3)}{\\cancel{w}}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{w(w-3)}}{\\cancel{w(w-3)}} & =\\frac{w\\cdot \\cancel{(w-3)}}{\\cancel{w-3}} \\\\ 2w-6+3 & =w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2w-6+3 & =w \\\\ w & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $w=3$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7caf21fb-c93a-5863-b6b5-6afc57e2a074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263497} {:answer/id #uuid "ec634806-4adb-5360-8110-ad5c92a2caa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263498} {:answer/id #uuid "26322217-e385-59d6-b13d-c7549234daec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263499} {:answer/id #uuid "c793fbb8-4d50-5ac8-a792-58fbd6e33b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263500} {:answer/id #uuid "92d3df67-6522-554b-ae7b-3ff51420886c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c793fbb8-4d50-5ac8-a792-58fbd6e33b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263500} :answer-field/id #uuid "4847b6ee-4a64-5ac9-9971-666494c84661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6946e332-e85c-5ad0-9c19-7c33272fdc4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98716" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{4}{x(x+2)}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,\\,x+2,\\,x(x+2))=x(x+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=0$ and $x=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ x(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}) & =x(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2x(x+2)}{x}-\\frac{x(x+2)}{x+2} & =\\frac{4x(x+2)}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cancel{x}\\cdot (x+2)}{\\cancel{x}}-\\frac{x\\cdot \\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{x(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x(x+2)}} \\\\ 2(x+2)-x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)-x & =4 \\\\ 2x+4-x & =4 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4106d58b-fc5f-5339-867f-e7ba5084ec61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263504} {:answer/id #uuid "d71acda6-4d27-5c83-9df8-41358ba4ba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263505} {:answer/id #uuid "ea085f83-9c3d-5b64-9da7-e3eba804c469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263506} {:answer/id #uuid "32afa4a5-c1ab-5c03-b40d-dcffd66ef419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263507} {:answer/id #uuid "de95c192-e13c-5802-ae8a-b77d8bfa1af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea085f83-9c3d-5b64-9da7-e3eba804c469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263506} :answer-field/id #uuid "e889e05b-38d1-529f-b983-866e79fbc471" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee49a3d7-fb85-5e79-860f-a66e5da04f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98718" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{{N}^{2}+N}=\\frac{1}{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{N(N+1)}=\\frac{1}{N}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(N,\\,N+1,\\,N(N+1))=N(N+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $N=0$ and $N=-1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{N(N+1)} & =\\frac{1}{N} \\\\ N(N+1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{N(N+1)}) & =N(N+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{N} \\\\ \\frac{2N(N+1)}{N+1}-\\frac{N(N+1)}{N(N+1)} & =\\frac{N(N+1)}{N} \\\\ \\frac{2N\\cdot \\cancel{(N+1)}}{\\cancel{N+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{N(N+1)}}{\\cancel{N(N+1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{N}\\cdot (N+1)}{\\cancel{N}} \\\\ 2N-1 & =N+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $N,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2N-1 & =N+1 \\\\ N & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne 0$ and $2\\ne -1,$ we conclude that $N=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $N=2.$"}]}
